--TEST--
a `#[Fixture]` is built once for its class and injected into each test declaring its type, `rule:testing/fixtures`
--RUN--
test
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:testing/core-test-fixture-built-once-injected-by-type
// `rule:testing/fixtures`: a fixture is built **once, in the parent isolate**, and
// injected into each test that declares a parameter of its type — resolution
// is by type, settled while compiling. So this case is the runner's own report
// (`--RUN--` above), a fixture's value being observable nowhere else.
//
// § 2 is why every count below is *stamped into the value* rather than read
// out of a `static` inside a test: the fixture bodies run in the parent and
// each test runs in an isolate of its own, so a counter a body incremented is
// not something a test can see. What crosses is the fixture's own graph, so
// what a body knew when it ran travels in a field of what it returned.
use Core\Test;
use Core\Test\Fixture;

final class Schema {
    public string $name;
    // Which build of a `Schema` this one was, across the whole run.
    public int $build;
    // How many `Cache` fixtures had been built when this one was.
    public int $caches;

    public function constructor(string $name, int $build, int $caches): void {
        $this->name = $name;
        $this->build = $build;
        $this->caches = $caches;
    }
}

final class Repo {
    public string $tag;
    // Which build of a `Repo` this one was, across the whole run.
    public int $build;

    public function constructor(Schema $schema, int $build): void {
        $this->tag = "repo of " . $schema->name;
        $this->build = $build;
    }
}

final class Cache {}

// How many times each fixture body was entered. Every one of these is read and
// written in the *parent*, where the bodies run; a test reads the number off
// the value it was handed instead.
final class Ledger {
    public static int $schemas = 0;
    public static int $repos = 0;
    public static int $caches = 0;
}

// Class order is the roster's sorted order, so this class is reported first
// however the file is written.
final class OtherTest {
    // The roster is the *class's* own: this supplies `Schema` too, and this
    // class's test is handed this one rather than `RepoTest`'s.
    #[Fixture]
    public static function schema(): Schema {
        Ledger::$schemas = Ledger::$schemas + 1;
        return new Schema("orders", Ledger::$schemas, Ledger::$caches);
    }

    // Nothing that runs asks for a `Cache`, which is what the count below is
    // about.
    #[Fixture]
    public static function cache(): Cache {
        Ledger::$caches = Ledger::$caches + 1;
        return new Cache();
    }

    // § 20's `skip:` never constructs the test, so the fixture it alone asks
    // for is never built — setup for a test the author asked to skip is work
    // nobody wanted. It is declared **first** so that the stamp two tests down
    // is evidence: a skip that had been ignored would build this class's
    // `Cache` ahead of its `Schema`, and that `Schema` would carry a `caches`
    // of 1.
    #[Test(skip: "the only test asking for a `Cache`")]
    public function itIsSkippedAndItsFixtureWithIt(Cache $cache): void {
        Test::assertSame(1, 2);
    }

    #[Test]
    public function itIsHandedItsOwnClassesFixture(Schema $schema): void {
        Test::assertSame($schema->name, "orders");
    }

    #[Test]
    public function aSkippedTestsFixtureIsNotBuilt(Schema $schema): void {
        Test::assertSame($schema->caches, 0);
        Test::assertSame($schema->build, 1);
    }
}

final class RepoTest {
    // § 8's own worked example, spelled as that section writes it.
    #[Fixture]
    public static function schema(): Schema {
        Ledger::$schemas = Ledger::$schemas + 1;
        return new Schema("users", Ledger::$schemas, Ledger::$caches);
    }

    // § 8's last sentence: a fixture may itself declare fixture parameters,
    // which resolve the same way — so this one is built *after* `schema`,
    // whatever order the two are written in.
    #[Fixture]
    public static function repo(Schema $schema): Repo {
        Ledger::$repos = Ledger::$repos + 1;
        return new Repo($schema, Ledger::$repos);
    }

    #[Test]
    public function itIsHandedWhatItsTypeNames(Schema $schema): void {
        Test::assertSame($schema->name, "users");
        Test::assertSame($schema->build, 2);
    }

    // Two parameters, resolved independently and passed in written order —
    // the chained fixture beside the one it was itself built from.
    #[Test]
    public function itIsHandedEachInOrder(Repo $repo, Schema $schema): void {
        Test::assertSame($repo->tag, "repo of users");
        Test::assertSame($schema->name, "users");
        Test::assertSame($schema->build, 2);
    }

    // A test that declares no parameter is untouched by any of it.
    #[Test]
    public function itAsksForNothing(): void {
        Test::assertSame(1, 1);
    }

    // The counts, asserted as an invariant across the sweep rather than read
    // off one line: every test of this class that asks for a `Schema` is handed
    // the **second** build in the run, so a fixture rebuilt per test would fail
    // here and two lines above while every other line still passed. Two rather
    // than one because each class built its own — one roster per class, one
    // build per roster.
    #[Test]
    public function itWasBuiltOnceForItsClass(Repo $repo, Schema $schema): void {
        Test::assertSame($schema->build, 2);
        Test::assertSame($repo->build, 1);
    }
}
--EXPECTF--
  OtherTest
    - itIsSkippedAndItsFixtureWithIt   %f ms
      skipped: the only test asking for a `Cache`
    ✓ itIsHandedItsOwnClassesFixture   %f ms
    ✓ aSkippedTestsFixtureIsNotBuilt   %f ms
  RepoTest
    ✓ itIsHandedWhatItsTypeNames       %f ms
    ✓ itIsHandedEachInOrder            %f ms
    ✓ itAsksForNothing                 %f ms
    ✓ itWasBuiltOnceForItsClass        %f ms

  0 failed, 6 passed, 1 skipped, 0 flaky in %d ms
